      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that fits inside a rectangle with sides of lengths 10 and 11, we need to determine the longest side that can fit within the rectangle. The longest side that can fit in the rectangle will be the minimum of the lengths of the sides of the rectangle. In this case, the longest side that can fit in the rectangle is 10. Therefore, the longest side of the equilateral triangle must be less than or equal to 10.\n\nNext, we will use the property of equilateral triangles and the fact that the area of an equilateral triangle is proportional to the square of its side length. The formula for the area of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nWe want to maximize this area. Since the side length \\(s\\) must be 10, we substitute \\(s = 10\\) into the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 10^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 100 = 25\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThus, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{25\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
