      "text": "[Solution]\nTo find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle inscribed in a rectangle with side lengths of 10 and 11, consider placing the rectangle either horizontally or vertically with its smaller side vertical, because this setup allows for the triangle to cover more area.\n\n1. **Horizontal Configuration:** When the triangle is horizontal, one of its vertices will be at one of the shorter sides of the rectangle (i.e., the vertical side), and its base will run along the top horizontal side. This configuration is not possible because the triangle must fit entirely within the rectangle without extending beyond its edges.\n\n2. **Two Triangles Configuration:** Now, place the triangle vertically, so that one vertex is at the bottom vertex of the rectangle, and its sides will be tangent to the two vertical sides of the rectangle. This provides room for the triangle to extend into the top horizontal side of the rectangle, as shown below:\n   ```\n        /\\\n       / |\n      /  |\n     /___|\n    /    <- Triangle\n```\n   Here, one side of the triangle (from vertex 'A' to 'F') is tangent to the 10-unit vertical line, another side from 'F' to 'S', and another from 'B' to 'Z' to 'S', and lastly, the hypotenuse from 'D' to 'X'.\n\n3. **Finding Hypotenuse Length:** The hypotenuse can be found using the Pythagorean theorem since the rectangle is a semicircle with radii of 5 and 5. To find the hypotenuse length (let's denote it as c), note that it splits the right triangle into two smaller triangles: one with legs 5 (vertical) and 10 (the entire length of the rectangle). Using the hypotenuse's formula for a right triangle (c² = a² + b²), we get:\n     \\[\n     c^2 = 5^2 + 10^2 \\\\\n      c^2 = 25 + 100 \\\\\n      c^2 = 125 \\\\\n      c = \\sqrt{125}\n     \\]\n\n4. The area of an equilateral triangle can use the following formula:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}(side \\; length)^2}{4}\n   \\]\n   Given that the hypotenuse 'c' is the side length 'S' of the triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} {(\\sqrt{125})}^2}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\times 125}{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. Therefore, after simplifying:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{125\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\nThe physical aspect requires us to interpret the maximum area as a structurally possible form represented in square units (not integral). Thus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{125\\sqrt{3}}{4}}\n\\]",
