      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that fits inside a rectangle with side lengths \\(10\\) and \\(11\\), we need to consider the possible orientations of the equilateral triangle relative to the rectangle. The key is to maximize the distance between the center of the triangle and the vertex of the triangle that is farthest from the rectangle.\n\n### Case 1: Equilateral Triangle Smaller than Rectangle\nThe largest possible equilateral triangle that can fit inside a rectangle with side lengths \\(10\\) and \\(11\\) has a side length equal to the length of the shorter side of the rectangle, which is \\(10\\). The area of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nPlugging in \\(s = 10\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 10^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 100 = 25\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: Equilateral Triangle Including Rectangle\nFor a larger equilateral triangle, one of its vertices will extend beyond one of the sides of the rectangle. However, the maximum such triangle must still satisfy the constraints derived from the rectangle's dimensions. If the triangle is oriented such that two of its sides are along the sides of the rectangle and the remaining two sides are diagonal (skipping one of the sides), the triangle's area is maximized when its height and base fit within the context of the rectangle.\n\nThe maximum side length of such an equilateral triangle can be derived by considering the right triangle formed by half the height of the equilateral triangle and the distance from the center of the triangle to the side of the rectangle. For an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) and height from the base to the opposite vertex, the height \\(h\\) and the distance \\(d\\) from the center to the sides of the rectangle can be calculated as follows:\n\nThe height \\(h\\) of an equilateral triangle is:\n\\[\nh = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s\n\\]\n\nSince the height of the equilateral triangle must be no more than the height of a right triangle formed by the height of the triangle and the distance \\(d\\), we have:\n\\[\nh \\leq 11\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\leq 11 \\implies s \\leq \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{3}} \\approx 11.547\n\\]\n\nThe maximum area of the equilateral triangle in this scenario would be:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2 \\approx \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left(\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times \\frac{484}{3} = \\frac{484\\sqrt{3}}{12} = \\frac{242\\sqrt{3}}{6} = \\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion\nBy comparing the two cases, the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that fits inside the rectangle with side lengths \\(10\\) and \\(11\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{25\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
