      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "1. **Identify the maximum possible orientation of the triangle**: The maximum area of a triangle inscribed in rectangle $ABCD$ occurs when the triangle is oriented such that one side of the triangle lies along one of the sides of the rectangle and the other two vertices are on the other vertices of the rectangle.\n   \n    - Let's denote the vertices of the triangle as points on the vertices of the rectangle.\n    - Let $x$ be the distance between the top vertices of the triangle \n$$D,M,$$\nwhere $D$ and $M$ are top-left and top-right vertices of rectangle $ABCD$, respectively.\n  \n    - In this configuration, one vertex of the equilateral triangle will be at the bottom-right corner of the rectangle and the other vertices will be situated symmetrically above $M$ as in Figure 1.\n\n2. **Calculate the height of the equilateral triangle**: The distance between the top of the rectangle ($B$) to the top of the triangle ($M$) is equal to $x$.\n    \n    - Hence, the height of the equilateral triangle is equal to $x$.\n   \n    - The side of an equilateral triangle can be represented by the formula:\n    $$\n    s = \\frac{x\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n    $$\n    \n3. **Calculate the area of the triangle using the height $h$**: We know that the height of a triangle can be calculated as: \n    $$\n    h = \\frac{1}{2}a \\times b\n    $$\n    in the case of an equilateral triangle, where $a$ and $b$ are the side lengths of the triangle:\n    Therefore, in this problem,$$h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times s, \\quad\\text{where} \\quad s = x\\sqrt{3}/2$$\n\n    - Thus we combine the above to obtain the equation:\n$$Area = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times s$$\n\n4. **Substituting $s$:** We find $s$ equals $x$:\n$$Area = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times x \\sqrt{3}/2$$\n5. **Approximate the area with known trigonometric values**: We simplify the equation by multiplying and dividing by $1/2$:\n\n    $$\n    Area = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times \\frac{3}{4} \\times s\n    $$\n    Assuming $x$ is small compared to $10$, and substituting $s$ = $x\\sqrt{3}$, and also assuming the values for simplification:\n    $$\n    Area \\approx \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 11 \n    $$\n6. **Conclude with the final value**: Evaluate the equation:\n\n    $$\n    Area \\approx \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 11 = 55\n    $$\n\n### Conclusion: $\\boxed{55}$",
